  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 127 AM EDT Sun Aug 30 2026\n\nCurrent radar and satellite depict widespread stratiform rain with a \nband of moderate rain at the northern most tip of a LLJ reaching \ninto northern MI. Surface rain guage reports show at most one to two \ntenths under this more moderate precipitation. Deep moisture is \npresent over the western Great Lakes region, thanks to a plume of \nPacific moisture streaming into the northern Plains over the Rockies \nand a more shallow and narrow Gulf tap moving over the central \nplains (towards WI). In the upper levels, a positively tilted upper \ntrough over the Pac NW bottoms out over the SoCal coast. This \nfeature has broken a larger upper ridge down and moved it over the \ncentral Rockies and plains. A focused jet over WY/MT has initiated a \nfew lee side lows over the Dakotas (which has driven the shallow \nGulf moisture transport towards the northern plains). As another lee \nside low spins over SD this morning, the surface warm front remains \ndraped over southern MN and WI with a weak extent into the southern \nL.P. of MI. The warm sector aloft is reaching farther north, and \nhints of this is seen with blossoming showers over central MI \n(proven by the presence of elevated instability within the LLJ).\nThe surface cyclone will weaken and drift to the east today. As\ntemperatures south of the warm front rise this afternoon, a 10F\ndegree temp gradient at the surface should set up between the \nnorthern and southern L.P. with the actual boundary resting \nsomewhere near M-55. As elevated instability lingers and lower \nlevel convergence forms today, quasi- zonal flow aloft will \ntrack waves of showers and rain bands from west to east. \nSoundings depict warm rain processes with WCD of 15 kft \npossible. \n\nAs the moist airmass continues tonight, the LLJ will strengthen and \nrefocus. By this time, the upper closed low will be slowly \ntracking across the international border (near MT). A stronger \njet over MT/WY will help initiate another surface low over \nSD/MN. Guidance keeps this farther north than the previous, \nwhich should keep the LLJ pointed towards northern MI. Another \nband of moderate rain will track across northern MI late this \nevening. The main difference will be a more defined presence of \nthe warm front draped over northern MI from west to east. A \ncouple hundred J/kg of elevated instability will be present \n(especially farther west), however more moderate rain will have \nan easier time forming along the frontal boundary. Off and on \nlight to moderate rain will continue into early Monday morning with\npockets of more steady moderate rain due to training along the \nforcing mechanism (front). Low chances for thunder due to the \nlower instability present. \n\nShower chances will shift to the north during the day on Monday\nas the upper closed low lifts into CAN and drags a trailing \ncold front with it over the U.P. Drier conditions and a brief \nreduction in temperatures will be seen Tuesday. The warm sector \nquickly returns early Wednesday ahead of another system moving \nacross the northern plains. This keeps temperatures above normal\nand rain chances in the forecast for the end of the work week. \n\nExpected Rain Amounts: Widespread half of an inch to an inch is\nexpected over northern lower by late Monday, with a swath of 1 \nto 2\" along and south of M- 72. Locally higher amounts will be \npossible where moderate rain can train over this 36 hour window.\nThere is moderate to high confidence (75%) in the bands of \nmoderate rain producing 1 to 2\" due to the deep moisture present\nand thus warm rain processes, as well as the forcing features \n(nose of the LLJ and along the warm frontal boundary). Most CAMs\ndo not overlap these forcing features for long periods of time,\nhowever an outlier or two does (and the resulting rain amounts \nare very high). Those very high amounts are not a likely outcome\ndue to the movement and shifts of the forcing features.",
